    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you provide a comparative analysis between signalized intersections and modern roundabout designs in terms of vehicle throughput and delay mitigation? I'm specifically looking at how these two configurations handle peak-hour congestion and the concept of \"flow friction\" in high-density urban environments.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Research suggests that modern roundabouts can generally outperform signalized intersections in terms of vehicle throughput and delay mitigation, especially during peak hours. Roundabouts are designed to reduce flow friction by allowing continuous flow and eliminating stop signs, traffic signals, and lane changes, resulting in shorter average travel times. In contrast, signalized intersections can create bottlenecks and reduce flow capacity due to lane changes, turning movements, and intersection phasing. Analytical studies have shown that roundabouts can achieve 10-20% higher vehicle throughput and reduced delay times, particularly in high-density urban environments.",
      "source": "target",
