    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go into more detail regarding the \"flow friction\" part? I'm particularly interested in the unpredictable elements—those sudden drops in efficiency or the irregular disruptions that don't always align with the standard mathematical models of capacity. What causes those specific types of systemic friction in a high-density roundabout?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Flow friction in roundabouts refers to the delays and inefficiencies caused by unpredictable events and disruptions, such as pedestrian crossings, bicycle traffic, and slow-moving drivers, which can occur without signaling a direct stop. These unanticipated elements disrupt the smooth flow of traffic, even in the absence of traffic signals. Studies have identified several sources of flow friction in roundabouts, including across-priority turns, concurrent lane changes, reduced lane width, and sudden directional changes. These irregularities can lead to noticeable delays, particularly during peak hours, and make it challenging to apply simple geometric capacity models to accurately predict flow capacity.",
      "source": "target",
