      "content": "Research suggests that psychological and social factors, such as driver uncertainty, anxiety, and spatial awareness, can also contribute to the sensitivity to disruptions in flow in roundabouts. For example, drivers may experience sudden feelings of unease or uncertainty when navigating complex or unfamiliar roundabouts, particularly when attempting to merge onto or exit the roundabout. Additionally, even a single slow or hesitant driver, like a cyclist attempting to merge into a roundabout, can cause a ripple effect and disrupt the flow of traffic. These psychological and social factors can make it challenging to accurately model and predict the capacity of roundabouts, as they can have a disproportionate impact on the flow even when the immediate causes of disruption are minor or random.",
